+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-accountants-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+To keep this list trustworthy, every submission must meet all of these requirements:
+
+1. The tool must have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+2. The repository must be relevant to accountants, bookkeeping, audit, AP/AR, FP&A, finance operations, or document-heavy accounting workflows.
+3. The project must be open source. Closed SaaS products without a public GitHub codebase will be rejected.
+4. The entry must include:
+   - Repository URL
+   - One-sentence explanation of accountant relevance
+   - Stars count
+   - Primary language
+   - License
+   - Last commit date
+5. If a category is thin, note the gap honestly instead of stretching the definition.
+
+## Preferred Entry Format
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary based on the repository README, including what it does, why it matters for accountants, license, stars, language, and last commit date.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-01
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+## Pull Requests
+
+Please keep pull requests focused. Do not bundle unrelated formatting changes with new tools.
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer repositories with active maintenance or clear historical importance.
+- Do not submit duplicate repos under multiple names.
+- Do not submit products that require proprietary cloud accounts if the public GitHub repo is only a thin SDK.
+- If the tool is not directly accounting-specific, explain the accounting workflow clearly.
